README for sdlsc
Bryan E. Topp <betopp@betopp.com> 2024

This is the SDL System-Call Shim library, "sdlsc".

It can be used to build an otherwise PVMK-focused application, on a normal desktop.

The sdlsc library contains an "sc.h" header that is used in place of the normal "sc.h" system-call header.

It contains a C source file, "sdlsc.c", which implements the functions in this header.

Together, this provides an implementation of the PVMK audio/video/input system calls, on top of SDL.

Build your application with:

	CFLAGS += -I<pvmk-sdk>/sdlsc/include -DUSE_SDLSC=1
	CFLAGS += $(shell sdl2-config --cflags)
	LIBS   += -lm $(shell sdl2-config --libs)

and compile sdlsc.c along with your own sources.

Threading, and the one line it asks of you
------------------------------------------
PVMK has a scanout engine that runs by itself: a game hands a buffer to
_sc_gfx_flip() and carries on, and flip's return value says which buffer is on
screen and therefore safe to draw into next. That is what makes double and
triple buffering possible.

SDL has a matching but stricter rule: video is set up on, and belongs to,
whichever thread called SDL_InitSubSystem(SDL_INIT_VIDEO). SDL 3 states it
outright ("We initialize video on the main thread. On Apple platforms this is a
requirement. On other platforms, this is the definition"), and SDL 2 says the
same of renderers in SDL_render.h. On Win32 there is a second reason: a window
belongs to the thread that created it, and only that thread can pump its
messages. So SDL video cannot be driven from a thread of the shim's choosing.

There are two ways to run, and both keep every SDL call on the main thread:

  Inline - no change to your program. Each system call does its own SDL work on
  the calling thread, which for a PVMK program is the process main thread. flip
  enqueues the frame and then presents it immediately, returning the buffer that
  was on screen before this flip. That is the same contract the kernel offers,
  so double and triple buffering work unchanged. Presenting is vertical-synced,
  so the application is paced by the display instead of running away from it.

  Pumped - make the body of main() one line:

	int app_main(int argc, const char **argv)	//your program, as usual
	{
		...
	}

	int main(int argc, const char **argv)
	{
		return _sc_shim_main(app_main, argc, argv);
	}

  (for an entry point taking no arguments, use _sc_shim_main(app_main)).
  _sc_shim_main() is a plain call on PVMK - see libsc's sc.h - and under the
  shim it gives the real main thread to the scanout/event pump and runs
  app_main() on a worker thread. The scanout then stays asynchronous, exactly
  like the console: flip enqueues, returns the displayed buffer, and does not
  block; there is one pending slot, as in the kernel, so an application that
  flips faster than the display drops frames rather than slowing down.

An application never calls SDL itself and never needs to know which mode it is
in. Nothing in the shim requires the one-liner; without it you get inline mode.

Headless mode
-------------
For automated runs - build machines, CI, watching for crashes - the shim can run
with no video at all. Nothing here changes the API; it is all environment
variables, so a PVMK application stays a drop-in replacement:

  SDLSC_HEADLESS=1      No SDL video: no window, no textures, no present.
                        Frames are still accounted for, and can be dumped.
                        (If SDL video cannot start at all, the shim prints a
                        note and goes headless by itself, so a machine with no
                        display still runs the application.)

  SDLSC_FRAMES=N        Exit(0) after N frames have been displayed. This is the
                        main automation hook: deterministic termination.

  SDLSC_DUMP=DIR        Write every displayed frame to DIR/frameNNNNNN.ppm, as
                        binary PPM (P6), no image library needed. The directory is
                        created if it does not exist.
  SDLSC_DUMP_EVERY=N    Only every Nth frame. Default 1.

  SDLSC_INPUT=FILE      Scripted input. One line per change:

                             # frame   buttons
                             30        a
                             45        a,up
                             60        -

                        Buttons: up, down, left, right, a, b, c, x, y, z,
                        start, mode. A change applies to the frame named, so it
                        is seen before the application draws that frame. Frames
                        here are frames of the run's clock: frames displayed when
                        running at real speed, frames of virtual time under
                        SDLSC_CLOCK=virtual. That matters for a program that sits
                        in an input loop without drawing anything - a "press
                        start" screen displays no frames at all, and its script
                        still has to arrive. Works windowed as well as headless.

  SDLSC_CLOCK=virtual   Nothing sleeps, and the clock advances one millisecond
                        per enquiry, so a run is deterministic and as fast as the
                        CPU allows. Use this for reproducible runs; without it
                        the clock is real time.

                        The clock deliberately does not depend on frames being
                        displayed. A program is entitled to wait for time to pass
                        before it draws anything - Doom's tic loop does exactly
                        that - and a clock that only advances when a frame is
                        displayed would have it waiting for a frame that only it
                        could produce; that was a hang. The price is that idle
                        time is charged per enquiry: a program that waits five
                        seconds of virtual time polls about five thousand times.

  SDLSC_TRACE=1         One line per displayed frame on stderr:
                            sdlsc: frame 12 mode 2 ticks 200 buttons 0x0000 hash 0x89abcdef
                        The hash is FNV-1a over the frame, so a regression shows
                        up in a log diff without keeping the images.

  SDLSC_VSYNC=0         Present without vertical sync (windowed runs only).
  SDLSC_NVM=PATH        NVM file to use instead of .nvm.bin, to keep test runs
                        from sharing saved data.
  SDLSC_AUDIO=off       Do not open an audio device at all. Headless runs
                        otherwise use SDL's dummy audio driver, so an
                        application's audio flow control still behaves.
  SDLSC_TITLE=TEXT      Window title (cosmetic).

So a full automated check of an example looks like:

  SDLSC_HEADLESS=1 SDLSC_CLOCK=virtual SDLSC_FRAMES=120 \
  SDLSC_INPUT=script.txt SDLSC_DUMP=out SDLSC_TRACE=1 ./example

which runs in milliseconds, exits 0, and leaves 120 frames and a log to compare.

Notes and limits
----------------
- _sc_gfx_flip() returns a 32-bit address, because PVMK is a 32-bit machine.
  On a 64-bit desktop a framebuffer above 4GB cannot be represented; the shim
  prints a warning once if it sees one, because the application then cannot
  recognise the buffer in the return value and its buffer bookkeeping will
  misbehave. Keep framebuffers static, or map them below 4GB.
- Text mode (graphics mode 0) clears the window; the shim has no access to the
  kernel's text buffer. _sc_print() writes to stdout instead, so an application
  can still report what it is doing.
- Input is the keyboard: WASD or the arrow keys for the d-pad, J/K/L for A/B/C,
  U/I/O for X/Y/Z, space/left-control/left-alt as extra A/B/C, Enter for Start,
  Backspace or Escape for Mode. There is no gamepad support yet.
- Sound is queued straight to SDL's audio device; _sc_snd_play() reports the
  number of bytes now enqueued, and mode 0 (silent) stops playback, as the
  kernel does.
